<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-reciprocals-of-infinite-limits" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Reciprocals of infinite limits
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove that a sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of negative real numbers <m> x_{ n } \lt 0 </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m> if and only if
      
      <md>
        \lim_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = 0
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      First suppose that <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m>, and let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. Then <m> -\frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon } \in \R </m> is a real number, and so there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        x_{ n } \lt -\frac{ 1 }{ \epsilon }
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. In particular,
      
      <md>
        d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } - 0 } = \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } } = -\frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } \lt \epsilon
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>, and so <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> 0 </m>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      Conversely, now suppose that <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> 0 </m>, and let <m> B \in \R </m> be a real number. If <m> B \leq 0 </m> is non-negative, then
      
      <md>
        x_{ n } \lt 0 \leq B
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>. On the other hand, if <m> B \lt 0 </m> is negative, then <m> -\frac{ 1 }{ B } \gt 0 </m> is a positive real number, and so there is a natural number <m> n_{ B } </m> so that
      
      <md>
        d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } \lt -\frac{ 1 }{ B }
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>. In particular,
      
      <md>
        x_{ n } = -\abs{ x_{ n } } = -\frac{ 1 }{ \abs{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } - 0 } } = -\frac{ 1 }{ d \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ x_{ n } } , 0 } } \lt B
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ B } </m>, and so <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> diverges to <m> -\infty </m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
